Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn a representation by predicting, in embedding space, the representation of a target part of an input from that of a context part~\citep{lecun2022path,assran2023ijepa,bardes2024vjepa}.
Unlike masked autoencoders~\citep{he2022mae} they reconstruct no pixels, and unlike contrastive methods they use no negatives, so their objective admits trivial minimisers: a constant encoder, or a representation that spans only a few directions, a dimensional collapse~\citep{jing2022dimensional} that degrades downstream performance~\citep{garrido2023rankme}.
JEPAs and related methods~\citep{grill2020byol,chen2021simsiam,caron2021dino,baevski2022data2vec} avoid collapse through design choices: predictor depth, width and learning rate, masking, a stop-gradient with an exponential moving average (EMA) target, and weight decay.

Theory explains parts of this but does not predict the outcome of training.
Analyses of linear BYOL, SimSiam and optimal predictors~\citep{tian2021understanding,wang2021directset,ponce2025dual,arbel2026peira} characterise the fixed points: a direction can survive only if its signal exceeds a static threshold set by weight decay.
With weight decay, however, the collapsed point is itself stable~\citep{tian2021understanding}, so the fixed points say which end states exist, not which one training reaches.
A recent analysis counts the directions that grow at initialisation, with the predictor held fixed~\citep{demelocosta2026drive}.
Neither predicts the rank that training ends with: on our held-out linear runs (\cref{sec:heldout}) the static threshold overestimates it by \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{} directions on average, and the measured rank exceeds the stability count, stated as an upper bound, in \DvdAlignedOver{} of runs (\cref{app:related}).


We argue that the missing ingredient is the decay of the predictor.
At a standard initialisation the encoder is small and the predictor of order one, and each direction $k$ of representation space grows at a net rate $g_k(p)$, drive minus decay, that is positive only while the predictor's gain $p$ lies in a window (\cref{fig:drive-decay}).
Weight decay moves the gain through and out of each window, and a direction survives only if it grows large enough to take over the predictor (capture) first.
This race has no fitted constants, and it predicts what the static picture misses: directions are lost at a weight decay far below the static threshold, the rank can rise and then fall with weight decay, and without predictor weight decay no direction survives.
It rests on one hypothesis, that collapse is decided early, near the collapsed solution; we test this premise too, and it holds in the linear models under constant weight decay but fails in a vision transformer.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item \textbf{The race criterion} (\cref{def:race}), new, built on near-collapse dynamics known in substance~\citep{tian2021understanding}, with a critical weight decay $\lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx h_k(p_0)/\log(p_0/a_0)$ far below the static threshold (\cref{sec:theory}).
\item \textbf{Registered held-out tests in linear JEPAs} (\cref{sec:experiments}): rank error \EoneBAlignedMae{}, against \EoneBAlignedWindowMae{} for counting the directions that grow at initialisation and \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{} for the static threshold, and \EtwoCycMae{} under masking; misses for deep predictors (corrected post hoc, re-tested), the EMA sign and nonlinear encoders under random masks.
\item \textbf{The reach of the account} (\cref{sec:ext}): modified forms that pass registered tests under AdamW (at a fixed horizon, not at convergence), with a layer-normalised target, in deep linear encoders with aligned factors and in a two-layer encoder under gradient descent, and failures elsewhere.
\item \textbf{A vision transformer} (\cref{sec:e8}): a race read early in training predicts the registered critical weight decays and their fall with training length, though not individual runs better than a step-0 rule, and weight-decay switches show that collapse in this ViT is not decided early.
\end{itemize}
